  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women&apos;s Economic Advancement for Collective Transformation (WEACT) in Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project works to enhance economic empowerment, well -being and inclusive economic growth for women in Ghana. The main issues it addresses include; gender -specific barriers for women&apos;s participation in the economic growth, legal and social barriers to women&apos;s economic growth, time -poverty of women, reduce unpaid work burden on women, lack of access to business and leadership skills etc. The project is on top of the agenda rising up to the challenge of addressing barriers to women economic empowerment in Ghana. In Ghana, women make up over 40% of the agricultural labour force &#x2014; a figure that is likely to become even higher as men increasingly exit farming and head to other forms of employment (Oxfam, System Innovation for Women&apos;s Economic Empowerment (SIWEE) &#x2014; Internal report for the inception research in Ghana, 2017). Yet women face many complex barriers, such as prohibitive social norms or restricted access to productive resources, patriarchal structures, the traditional division of labour at the household level and lack of education for women (Vignera and Holmes, Overseas Development Institute (ODI), 2009). As such, in this intervention Oxfam alongside its civil society, governmental and corporate partners will take a systemic approach to tackle the barriers women face seeking to increase the agency and leadership of women to be economically empowered through multiple entry points. This means working collaboratively across stakeholders (women&apos;s rights organizations, governments, private sector, civil society, primary change agents, etc.) bringing together the strengths and areas of influence of all these actors to create changes which are transformational in nature and will shift attitudes and behaviours across the agricultural sector in promotion of women economic empowerment (WEE). Over the next 5 years, Oxfam aims to contribute to enhancing economic empowerment, well -being and inclusive economic growth for women, including young women, in the shea and cocoa s</narrative>
  </description>
